;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; AUTHORED, not model-seeded. The drafting batch
;;; (scratch/pairs/draft_records.py, see draft_run.log) skipped
;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/append-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "append!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(append! lst ...) -> list"
  :summary "The arguments joined end to end by relinking their last cells, rather than by copying. Every argument but the last is destroyed."
  :explanation "append copies every argument except the last, because it must not disturb them. append! copies nothing: it walks to the end of each list and writes the next list into that last cell. The suite pins the sharing exactly at list.sld:339-349 -- the result is eq? to the first argument, and the cells of every later argument appear in the result unchanged. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that. Two details a caller will meet. Empty arguments are skipped, so (append! x y '() z) joins three lists and the empty one leaves no trace. And append! is NOT cheap: it must find each list's end, so it costs a full traversal of every argument but the last. What it saves is allocation, not time. Anyone reaching for it because \"the destructive one is O(1)\" has the wrong model -- that is what set-cdr! on a held last cell is for, and append! is the version that does not make you hold the cell."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(display (append! a b))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; a is the result; b is now a suffix of a.\n(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(define joined (append! a b))\n(display (list 'joined-is-a (eq? joined a) 'b-inside (eq? (cddr joined) b)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; empty arguments are skipped; later mutation of b shows through the result.\n(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(define c (list 5 6))\n(define joined (append! a b (list) c))\n(set-car! b 99)\n(display (list 'joined joined))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("Every argument but the last is destroyed. list.sld:339-349 asserts the result is eq? to the first argument and contains the later arguments' own cells."
             "The later arguments remain live inside the result. Mutating one of them afterwards changes the result too."
             "It costs a traversal of every argument but the last, so it is NOT constant time. It saves allocation only."
             "Empty arguments are skipped and contribute nothing, including no cell to relink."
             "If the same list is passed twice you have built a cycle, and anything that walks the result will not stop.")
  :drawbacks ("Passing a list you did not build makes the caller's list a suffix of yours, which is action at a distance in both directions."
             "The traversal cost surprises people who chose it for speed; the pure append costs the same walk plus one allocation."
             "The aliasing rules out reuse: an argument to append! can never be appended again.")
  :usecases ("Assembling a large result from freshly-built segments inside a single producer."
             "Accumulator patterns where each segment is built, appended and never referred to again."
             "Avoiding a copy of a very large prefix when the prefix is provably single-owner.")
  :related ("append"
             "concatenate!"
             "append-reverse!"
             "reverse!"
             "set-cdr!")
  :learn (:concept "Relinking versus copying, and why relinking is not free"
          :prerequisites ("append" "set-cdr!" "last-pair" "eq?")
          :progression "Understand what append copies and why. Then look at where append! writes, and count the traversals -- the count is the surprise.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; conformance evidence scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:339-349; signature derived from the append record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat. Drafted 2026-08-03; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(display (append! a b))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a1 (list 1 2))\n(define b1 (list 3 4))\n(define a2 (list 1 2))\n(define b2 (list 3 4))\n(display (equal? (append a1 b1) (append! a2 b2)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(define joined (append! a b))\n(display (list 'result-is-first-arg (eq? joined a) 'second-arg-embedded (eq? (cddr joined) b)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is assembling one long list out of many freshly-built segments. Candidates: append (copies every segment but the last, source lists all survive), a fold with cons over the reversed segments (one allocation per element, no traversal of ends), or append! (no allocation, every segment but the last destroyed). The measurement that decides this is allocation pressure, and the fact that changes it is ownership -- if any segment came from outside the producer, append! is wrong regardless of what the profiler says."
     :explanation "eng: the segments become one list and the segment variables are no longer independent. math: append! agrees with append as a value for every sequence of proper lists. cs: the eq? checks are the observable difference, and they are what makes a later mutation of any segment visible in the result."
     :meta "The performance argument for append! is narrower than people assume: it removes allocation and keeps the traversal. Reaching for it to remove the traversal is a misread.")
    (:eng "Know which cost you are removing. Removing allocation and removing traversal are different purchases."
     :math "append and append! are the same function of their arguments; only the survivors differ."
     :cs "Building a cycle by passing the same list twice is the sharp edge, and nothing warns you.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Joining sequences by connecting the end of one to the start of the next, instead of copying them into a new sequence."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(display (append! (list 1 2) (list 3 4)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "coupling two rakes of wagons" :code "(define front (list 'loco 'car1))\n(define back (list 'car2 'van))\n(display (append! front back))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "splicing two spools of tape" :code "(define reel-a (list 'a1 'a2))\n(define reel-b (list 'b1 'b2))\n(display (append! reel-a reel-b))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, the last cell of one list is made to point at the next. Coupling two rakes is the same act performed with a coupler: no wagons are built, and afterwards there is one train, not two. Splicing tape joins two reels into one and there is no way to hand someone reel-a afterwards. All three share a join that consumes the separateness of the parts."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever joining is physical rather than descriptive -- where after the join the parts no longer independently exist.")
    (:eng "A physical join destroys the parts. A described join does not. Know which one your verb is."
     :math "Concatenation is associative, which is why the multi-argument form is well-defined without bracketing."
     :cs "Walking to the end of each segment is the hidden cost, and it is why an accumulator that keeps the last cell beats repeated appending.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(define j (append! a b))\n(display (list 'joined j 'len (length j)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (assoc-holds?)\n  (let ((l (append (list 1) (append (list 2) (list 3))))\n        (r (append (append (list 1) (list 2)) (list 3))))\n    (equal? l r)))\n(display (assoc-holds?))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(define j (append! a b))\n(set-car! b 99)\n(display (list 'mutating-b-changed-the-result j))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For proper lists l1..ln, append! returns a list equal? to (append l1 ... ln). The result is eq? to the first non-empty argument, and the cells of every later argument are present in the result unchanged, so a subsequent mutation of any argument is visible through the result."
     :invariant "forall proper l1..ln. equal?(append!(l1,...,ln), append(l1,...,ln)) and eq?(append!(l1,...,ln), first-non-empty(l1,...,ln))"
     :meta "The pattern is value-equality with structural sharing. What would falsify the claim: disagreement with append, a fresh list where the first argument was required, or a later argument being copied instead of embedded. The mutation program is the sharpest test -- an implementation that copies passes every equal? check and fails this one.")
    (:eng "The mutation-after-join check is the one that catches a copying implementation. Nothing else does."
     :math "Associativity means the n-ary form needs no bracketing rule, which is why SRFI-1 spells it variadic."
     :cs "Structural sharing is the feature and the hazard, and they cannot be separated.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (assemble! . segments)\n  (if (null? segments) (list) (apply append! segments)))\n(display (assemble! (list 1 2) (list 3) (list 4 5)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (flatten-once xss)\n  (apply append xss))\n(display (flatten-once (list (list 1 2) (list 3) (list 4 5))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (build-with-tail-pointer items)\n  (let loop ((rest items) (acc (list)))\n    (if (null? rest) (reverse acc) (loop (cdr rest) (cons (car rest) acc)))))\n(display (build-with-tail-pointer (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :base "append! joins lists by writing each one into the last cell of the one before it."
     :emergent "eng: a variadic assembler, which is what apply plus append! buys you. math: concatenate is exactly this with the segments already in a list, which is why SRFI-1 ships both and why concatenate! is not redundant. cs: the accumulate-and-reverse idiom is the version that never appends at all -- it is O(n) total where repeated appending is O(n squared), and it is the right answer far more often than either append or append!."
     :meta "The family is list assembly. What emerges is that the interesting choice is usually not append versus append!, but whether to append at all -- cons-and-reverse beats both, and reaching for a destructive append is often a sign the accumulator was built the wrong way round.")
    (:eng "The best fix for a slow append is usually to stop appending, not to append destructively."
     :math "Repeated concatenation is quadratic; accumulation with a single final reversal is linear."
     :cs "cons-and-reverse allocates more and runs faster, which is a useful corrective to allocation-counting.")
  )
)
